{
  "message": "**Question:** Which unit defines a “character” for non-BMP Unicode characters and combining-character sequences when applying the 4000-character input guard and output length limits?\n\nWhy it matters: The chosen counting rule determines exactly which inputs and model outputs pass the length checks.\n\n**Recommended:** Option A - Count Unicode code points, matching Python string length behavior and avoiding the complexity of user-perceived grapheme counting.\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| A | Unicode code points (each combining mark counts separately; an emoji outside the basic multilingual plane counts as one) |\n| B | UTF-16 code units (some emoji count as two units) |\n| C | User-perceived characters (grapheme clusters; combining sequences count as one) |\n| D | UTF-8 bytes |\n| Short | Provide a different short answer (<=5 words) |\n\nYou can reply with the option letter (e.g., “A”), accept the recommendation by saying “yes” or “recommended”, or provide your own short answer.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "unit": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Character counting unit",
        "description": "Choose a counting rule. You may also answer yes/recommended or provide another short answer.",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "A",
            "title": "A — Unicode code points"
          },
          {
            "const": "B",
            "title": "B — UTF-16 code units"
          },
          {
            "const": "C",
            "title": "C — User-perceived characters (grapheme clusters)"
          },
          {
            "const": "D",
            "title": "D — UTF-8 bytes"
          }
        ],
        "default": "A"
      }
    }
  }
}

{
  "message": "**Question:** Which exact character set counts as a “key character” after `sk-` in the API-key pattern?\n\nWhy it matters: The set determines which ticket content and model replies are blocked by the secret-leakage guardrail.\n\n**Recommended:** Option B - ASCII letters and digits plus `_` and `-`; this covers common key-token characters, including hyphens in prefixed keys, without treating arbitrary punctuation as key content.\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| A | ASCII letters and digits only (`A-Z`, `a-z`, `0-9`) |\n| B | ASCII letters and digits, underscore, and hyphen (`A-Z`, `a-z`, `0-9`, `_`, `-`) |\n| C | Any printable ASCII character except whitespace |\n| Short | Provide a different short answer (<=5 words) |\n\nYou can reply with the option letter (e.g., “B”), accept the recommendation by saying “yes” or “recommended”, or provide your own short answer.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "charset": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "API-key character set",
        "description": "Choose which characters count after sk-. You may also answer yes/recommended or provide another short answer.",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "A",
            "title": "A — ASCII letters and digits"
          },
          {
            "const": "B",
            "title": "B — ASCII letters, digits, underscore, and hyphen"
          },
          {
            "const": "C",
            "title": "C — All printable ASCII except whitespace"
          }
        ],
        "default": "B"
      }
    }
  }
}

{
  "message": "**Question:** Does “secrets” in GR-005 include sensitive values beyond the specified card-number and API-key patterns, and, if so, which detectable forms are in scope?\n\nWhy it matters: Expanding secret detection changes the guardrail rules and evaluation cases, while an overly broad scope can reject ordinary ticket text.\n\n**Suggested:** Only the listed patterns - Keep the scope aligned with the concrete card-number and API-key checks already defined, including the literal `OPENAI_API_KEY` guard.\n\nFormat: Short answer (<=5 words). You can accept the suggestion by saying “yes” or “suggested”, or provide your own answer.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "forms": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Additional secret forms",
        "description": "Answer in 5 words or fewer, or accept the suggestion by entering yes/suggested.",
        "default": "Only the listed patterns"
      }
    }
  }
}

{
  "message": "**Question:** Does “up to 3 attempts” mean three attempts total or an initial attempt followed by up to three retries?\n\nWhy it matters: The definition sets the maximum model calls per evaluation case and affects when the case is marked `ERROR`.\n\n**Recommended:** Option A - Three attempts total, including the initial attempt; this gives a clear cap of two retries and avoids unintended extra calls.\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| A | Three attempts total: one initial attempt and up to two retries |\n| B | One initial attempt followed by up to three retries (four attempts total) |\n\nYou can reply with the option letter (e.g., “A”), accept the recommendation by saying “yes” or “recommended”, or provide your own short answer.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "attempts": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Attempt limit",
        "description": "Choose the maximum-attempt interpretation. You may also answer yes/recommended.",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "A",
            "title": "A — Three attempts total (initial + up to two retries)"
          },
          {
            "const": "B",
            "title": "B — Four attempts total (initial + up to three retries)"
          }
        ],
        "default": "A"
      }
    }
  }
}

{
  "message": "**Question:** When a ticket does not state whether a workaround exists, what evidence is sufficient to distinguish `medium` from `high` priority?\n\nWhy it matters: A consistent rule prevents otherwise similar tickets from receiving different priorities based on an unstated assumption.\n\n**Recommended:** Option B - Treat missing information as no proof that a workaround is unavailable; assign `high` only when the ticket explicitly establishes there is no workable alternative, and otherwise use `medium`.\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| A | If the main use is blocked, assume no workaround and assign `high` |\n| B | Require explicit evidence that no workable alternative exists for `high`; otherwise use `medium` |\n| C | Ask a clarifying question whenever workaround availability is unstated |\n\nYou can reply with the option letter (e.g., “B”), accept the recommendation by saying “yes” or “recommended”, or provide your own short answer.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "priority": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Priority evidence rule",
        "description": "Choose how to handle tickets that omit workaround availability. You may also answer yes/recommended.",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "A",
            "title": "A — Main-use blockage implies high"
          },
          {
            "const": "B",
            "title": "B — Explicit no-workaround evidence required for high"
          },
          {
            "const": "C",
            "title": "C — Ask a clarifying question"
          }
        ],
        "default": "B"
      }
    }
  }
}
